USOS CSMWrap patch 0022 (2026-10-19): USB disk service stage 2, hot-swap

Stage 2 of docs/usb9x-hotswap-design-2026-10-19.md (USOS repository): a
USB stick plugged in after boot, unplugged, or swapped is seen by the USB
disk service of patch 0021 (channel 1 of the helper core, USOSUSB.MPD on
Windows 95/98/98 SE/Me). SeaBIOS stays the only xHCI owner; the
controller is never reset.

Only with csmwrap.ini int13_boot_disk_only = true (patch 0014, written by
the Windows 9x flow), detected at POST through csm_bootdisk_hash():

- a fixed pool, 12 KiB from ZoneHigh at POST (E820 reserved, survives the
  OS): page 0 = the input context of the bring-up commands and the sector
  buffer of the media check, pages 1-2 = one device each (output device
  context, control pipe, two bulk pipes, a 512-byte descriptor buffer).
  Re-used on every plug: no runtime allocation, no leak;
- every xHCI controller is registered (up to 4) and stays running when
  POST found no device on it (upstream stops and frees it), so a stick can
  arrive on any root port;
- the port watch arms at the first channel 1 request (the OS client
  exists; from then on all 32-bit SeaBIOS code runs on the helper core):
  each root port is classified once (a UDS stick, a device POST set up such
  as the keyboard / mouse / a hub, connected without a device slot, or
  free). A port that is connected but has no slot (POST did not set it up,
  e.g. the X470 stick after a warm restart) is brought up like a new plug;
- about every 100 ms, only when neither channel has a request, the helper
  processes the events of each watched controller and reads PORTSC: CCS,
  and CSC directly or latched by xhci_process_events (which clears CSC on
  a Port Status Change event, so a fast unplug / replug is not lost);
  connect -> one watch period of debounce -> bring-up; unplug or a change
  latch on a UDS port -> teardown (Disable Slot, events drained, the
  slot's context pointer cleared, the pool chunk free) and the table entry
  reports GONE; one bring-up per pass, so HID polls get the core back;
- bring-up (own code from xHCI 1.2 and USB 2.0 chapter 9): port reset (USB2
  PR; a USB3 port only when its link did not train by itself), Enable
  Slot, Address Device, the device descriptor (8 bytes, Evaluate Context for
  another EP0 packet size, 18 bytes), the configuration descriptor (at
  most 512 bytes), the first bulk-only mass storage interface (alternate
  setting 0) with its bulk endpoints, SET_CONFIGURATION, one Configure
  Endpoint for both, GET MAX LUN (a stall = one LUN, EP0 reset). Anything
  else (a keyboard plugged in again, a hub, UAS only) is disabled and left
  until it is unplugged. The device table entry: an empty one or one whose
  stick is gone / ejected (never one INT 13h owns), a new generation
  (global, POST entries keep 1), so the miniport reports a media change;
- the next watch pass: INQUIRY, READ CAPACITY and the media check;
- EJECT now also disables the slot and frees the chunk; the port waits for
  a disconnect, the next plug is a new stick.

Every boot: the media check at a stick's first probe (sector 0, the MBR
partition types, the boot sector of the first partition or of sector 0
itself: FAT12/16/32, NTFS, exFAT, GPT protective, unknown); KiB counters
exact over many requests (the remainder is carried; 0021 rounded each
request down, so 512-byte writes counted 0).

The USOSUDSK record becomes version 2 (984 bytes, version 1 unchanged as
the first 768): watch passes, plugs, unplugs, failed plugs with the last
reason, the pool, per controller the ports, CCS after POST, ports with a
slot when the watch armed, CCS now; per device the media check (file
system code, partition types, flags, controller, POST or hot-plug, the
volume LBA, FNV-1a of sector 0); new events: plugged in, plug failed
(reason), connect, unplugged, connected without a slot, media, armed.

romlayout.S: the helper loop calls usos_uds_idle() when neither channel has
a request; it returns 1 once armed and the loop then spins with PAUSE
instead of MWAIT (which only wakes on the mailbox line). The armed flag is
a VARFSEG byte: the loop runs before SeaBIOS has set up its low memory.

ROM room (SeaBIOS build, usos16 4416 bytes free): seabios-config turns off
CONFIG_MEGASAS (LSI MegaRAID SAS) and CONFIG_USB_UHCI (UHCI exists only on
pre-2011 chipsets, which have a firmware CSM; xHCI, EHCI, OHCI, AHCI,
NVMe, SD, USB HID / MSC / UAS stay).

Without int13_boot_disk_only every path behaves as in 3.1.2-usos16 apart
from the media check, the exact KiB counters and the two drivers.

Own code. Applies after 0001-0010, 0012, 0014-0021, patch -p1 from the
top of the tree. Part of a MODIFIED CSMWrap made by the USOS project
(SeaBIOS LGPLv3).

